wall regex, matches the identity-self template text) across seeded AND TMCT_NO_SEED=1 sessions —
not string presence. No issue found.
(2) Read normalize.mjs + fuzzy.mjs directly, as briefed. There IS an existing word-contraction
mechanism — ask-vocab.mjs:257-272 CONTRACTIONS (already has the exact precedent: "ur"->"your" as
a standalone \b-bounded token, applied via normalize.mjs:31 CONTRACTION_RE) — but it is the WRONG
hook point: chat.mjs's conversationalTurn (chat.mjs:771) never calls normalizeQuery at all (chat.mjs
imports only COUNTERFACTUAL_RE from interpret/normalize.mjs, confirmed by grep) and operates
directly on raw/lowercased text; normalizeQuery is wired only into ask.mjs/interpret/pipeline.mjs's
code-graph question grammar. Adding "r"->"are","u"->"you" to the SHARED CONTRACTIONS table would
leak single-letter token rewrites into the code-graph grammar path too (a real collision-risk
regression the docstring's own "no guessing" discipline would flag), with zero effect on the
conversational lane it's meant to fix. CONCRETE FIX LOCATION: apply a small, LOCAL r/u-only
contraction pass inside chat.mjs's conversational lane itself, at the same prep step as
collapseRuns/fuzzyConversationalMatch (chat.mjs:737-742) — reusing that lane's existing
looksCodeish + ≤4-word guard as the safety perimeter, not the shared vocab table's broader surface.
Dry-run check: "waht r u" -> (r/u expansion) "waht are you" -> editDistance to "who are you"/"what
are you" pool entries is now 1 (h/a adjacent transposition, already supported by fuzzy.mjs's
Damerau-Levenshtein) at matching length — confirms the fix design closes the gap without touching
CONTRACTIONS.
(3) "hru" and "wyd" do NOT decompose under any word-contraction pass — they're glued (no space
between h/r/u or w/y/d), so a \b-token contraction regex structurally cannot split them; they need
a SEPARATE literal-shorthand table entry (same species as GREET's existing literal set), not the
r/u contraction mechanism. Flagging so the eventual fix doesn't conflate "spaced contraction"
(mechanism: token-substitution regex) with "glued shorthand" (mechanism: literal closed-set
lookup) into one over-reaching change.
